650: \begin{remark}\label{Remark:log^2}
651:    \textcolor{red}{As elaborated in Section~\ref{sec:InformalOnline}, Condition~\eqref{eq:CondInformal} is not only central to our lower bound analysis but also naturally motivated by the behavior of Karp’s algorithm. While Karp’s algorithm performs $O(n\log n)$ edge inspections overall, only $O(\log^2 n)$ of these are the edges contained in the final independent set. This naturally motivates a formulation of the future information budget in which at most $O(\log^2 n)$ of the future edges revealed have both endpoints in the final output $\mathcal{A}(G)$. An interesting direction for future work is to investigate whether practical online algorithms with look-ahead naturally satisfy Condition~\eqref{eq:CondInformal}, or whether similar sharp bounds can be established under alternative inspection budget constraints.}
655:\paragraph{Sharpness of Computational Threshold} Complementing Algorithm~\ref{Alg:Greedy}, our first main result establishes a sharp algorithmic lower bound against $c$-restricted online algorithms, for sufficiently small~$c$.
