773:We next delve into the role of sets $S_t$ of future edges. For simplicity, suppose that $\A$ probes the vertices in order $\{1,\dots,n\}$. Then, $S_t$ is allowed to contain vertex-pairs $\{i,j\}$ with $i\ge t+1$ or $j\ge t+1$, or both --- that is, involving vertices that have not yet been probed by $\A$. Including $S_t$ makes the algorithm stronger by providing additional information that would otherwise be unavailable at round $t$.  To see this, suppose that at round $t$, we observe $e(i,t) = 0$ for all $i\in\A_{t-1}(G)$ --- i.e., the current vertex $t$ is not adjacent to any vertex in the independent set constructed thus far. Ignoring $S_t$, the natural greedy decision would be to add $t$ to the independent set:
