\subsection{
Discussion of the Added Value of Future Edges $S_t$} 
\label{sec:ExceptionalSet}
We next delve into the role of sets $S_t$ of future edges. For simplicity, suppose that $\A$ probes the vertices in order $\{1,\dots,n\}$. Then, $S_t$ is allowed to contain vertex-pairs $\{i,j\}$ with $i\ge t+1$ or $j\ge t+1$, or both --- that is, involving vertices that have not yet been probed by $\A$. Including $S_t$ makes the algorithm stronger by providing additional information that would otherwise be unavailable at round $t$.  To see this, suppose that at round $t$, we observe $e(i,t) = 0$ for all $i\in\A_{t-1}(G)$ --- i.e., the current vertex $t$ is not adjacent to any vertex in the independent set constructed thus far. Ignoring $S_t$, the natural greedy decision would be to add $t$ to the independent set:
\[
\A_t(G) \leftarrow \A_{t-1}(G)\cup \{t\}.
\]
Now, suppose that $S_t$ reveals a large subset of future vertices $j_1,\dots,j_K\ge t+1$ such that: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item[(a)] $e(i,j_u)=0$ for all $i\in\A_{t-1}(G)$ and $\{i,j_u\}\in S_t$
    \item [(b)] $e(t,j_u)=1$ for all $u$.
\end{itemize}
In this case, including $t$ in the independent set --- which may have seemed optimal without the extra information $S_t$ --- would prevent us to ever include $j_1,\dots,j_K$ in future rounds. Thus, the ability to query future vertex-pairs allows the algorithm to make more globally informed decisions.



Condition~\eqref{eq:CondInformal} controls the total number of such future edges that are inside the final independent set. In what follows, we argue the sharpness of $\log_b^2(np)$ scaling by showing that: (a) for any $\epsilon>0$ and $c$ sufficiently small, online algorithms fail to identify an independent set of size $(1+\epsilon)\log_b(np)$; whereas (b) there exists an online algorithm with $c=3\epsilon$ that finds an independent set of this size.
