The paper’s hardness theorem applies only to restricted online algorithms on unconditioned \(G(n,p)\) instances—not to offline search or planted graphs ([Theorem 2.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.11450v2)). Its above-threshold construction obtains the witness through brute-force independent-set search, violating G. Planting a witness restores G and V but changes the distribution, leaving no Track A hardness theorem. Track B also lacks a shorter structural route than the mechanical search.

The full, quantified decision—including mechanical and compact-route costs—is in [REJECTED.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2504.11450/REJECTED.md).
